    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "26-29",
      "detail": "3rd in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The Blue Jays finally found some daylight, and for one night at least the offense looked like it had a pulse. <strong>Toronto's 8-1 thumping of Miami on May 26 snapped a two-game skid and gave the club a needed lift</strong> after a shaky stretch that had threatened to drag the whole operation under. At 26-29, third in the AL East, they are still buried 9.5 games out and sitting 8th in the playoff seed, so this is not a rescue mission yet - just evidence they can still land a punch. Jesús Sánchez set the tone with a first-career grand slam, while Ernie Clement and Yohendrick Piñango also went deep, and Kazuma Okamoto and George Springer chipped in RBI singles as Toronto piled up 12 hits. That kind of spread-the-wealth box score matters because this lineup has spent too many nights leaning on one or two bats and praying. The larger question is whether this was a one-night outburst or the start of something sturdier. <strong>They get Miami again Wednesday at 12:07 p.m. ET, and another clean start would make last night look less like a fluke and more like a turn</strong>.",
        "updated": "Updated May 27, 2026"
      },
